1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:07,550
Eighteen feet under the clear water off North Bimini, in the Bahamas, there is a line of giant stone blocks.

2
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:14,033
Flat, squarish, set edge to edge, running straight for half a mile, and then curling into a hook.

3
00:00:14,433 --> 00:00:20,324
In September nineteen sixty-eight, three divers swam over it and called it a pavement.

4
00:00:20,324 --> 00:00:24,161
Within months, a lot of people had a better name for it.

5
00:00:24,161 --> 00:00:25,600
The road to Atlantis.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,833
Because, twenty-eight years earlier, a man lying on a couch in a trance had said: expect it in sixty-eight and sixty-nine.

7
00:00:34,233 --> 00:00:35,008
A prophecy.

8
00:00:35,008 --> 00:00:35,502
A date.

9
00:00:35,502 --> 00:00:39,449
And a road of stone, exactly where he said Atlantis lay.

10
00:00:39,449 --> 00:00:42,833
If that is a coincidence, it is a very good one.

11
00:00:43,233 --> 00:00:47,247
So today we test it the way science is supposed to work.

12
00:00:47,247 --> 00:00:51,332
Two explanations: a lost civilization, or a natural rock.

13
00:00:51,332 --> 00:00:53,267
Each one makes predictions.

14
00:00:53,267 --> 00:00:56,923
If it's true, then something else must be true too.

15
00:00:56,923 --> 00:00:58,714
We check them one by one.

16
00:00:58,714 --> 00:00:59,933
Then, the result.

17
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:06,229
Prediction zero isn't ours.

18
00:01:06,229 --> 00:01:07,563
It's Edgar Cayce's.

19
00:01:07,563 --> 00:01:17,183
Known as the Sleeping Prophet, Cayce gave thousands of trance readings from the nineteen twenties until his death in nineteen forty-five.

20
00:01:17,183 --> 00:01:21,466
His foundation still keeps the transcripts in Virginia Beach.

21
00:01:21,866 --> 00:01:24,048
Bimini comes up again and again.

22
00:01:24,048 --> 00:01:31,616
In nineteen twenty-six, a reading called it the highest portion left above the waves of a once great continent.

23
00:01:31,616 --> 00:01:41,366
In nineteen thirty-three, another said Atlantean temples might yet be found under the slime of ages of sea water, near what is known as Bimini.

24
00:01:41,766 --> 00:01:53,314
Then, on the twenty-eighth of June, nineteen forty, reading nine fifty-eight dash three: Poseidia will be among the first portions of Atlantis to rise again.

25
00:01:53,314 --> 00:01:56,256
Expect it in sixty-eight and sixty-nine.

26
00:01:56,256 --> 00:01:57,433
Not so far away.

27
00:01:57,833 --> 00:02:08,821
On the second of September, nineteen sixty-eight, the zoologist Joseph Manson Valentine, the free diver Jacques Mayol and Robert Angove found the stones.

28
00:02:08,821 --> 00:02:11,263
Valentine knew the Cayce readings.

29
00:02:11,263 --> 00:02:13,633
The link was made almost at once.

30
00:02:14,033 --> 00:02:15,502
Expeditions followed.

31
00:02:15,502 --> 00:02:29,566
One in nineteen seventy-one included the Apollo fourteen astronaut Edgar Mitchell, the underwater photographer Dimitri Rebikoff, and, according to a magazine report, psychics from the Cayce foundation.

32
00:02:29,966 --> 00:02:31,774
So does prediction zero pass?

33
00:02:31,774 --> 00:02:33,083
Read the words again.

34
00:02:33,083 --> 00:02:34,330
Poseidia would rise.

35
00:02:34,330 --> 00:02:35,140
Nothing rose.

36
00:02:35,140 --> 00:02:45,364
The stones had been lying underwater all along, and the geologist Eugene Shinn wrote that the rows were known in the early nineteen sixties, before the famous date.

37
00:02:45,364 --> 00:02:52,533
When a prophecy can be fulfilled by finding something, instead of something happening, it was never much of a test.

38
00:02:52,933 --> 00:02:57,444
Prediction zero: a striking coincidence, but too vague to count.

39
00:02:57,444 --> 00:03:02,166
So let's give the Atlantis idea predictions that can actually fail.

40
00:03:02,566 --> 00:03:03,611
Prediction one.

41
00:03:03,611 --> 00:03:06,676
If this is Plato's Atlantis, it must be old.

42
00:03:06,676 --> 00:03:14,338
Plato placed the sinking about nine thousand years before Solon, roughly eleven and a half thousand years ago.

43
00:03:14,338 --> 00:03:18,100
Anything Atlanteans built should be at least that old.

44
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:24,639
Atlantis has been pinned to dozens of places, from the Eye of the Sahara to Antarctica.

45
00:03:24,639 --> 00:03:30,566
Here we test just one stretch of seabed, and this time the rock itself can be dated.

46
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:41,551
In nineteen seventy-seven, geologists drilled a core from one of the Bimini blocks and sent it to the radiocarbon laboratory at the University of Miami.

47
00:03:41,551 --> 00:03:45,033
The dates were published in nineteen seventy-nine.

48
00:03:45,433 --> 00:03:52,002
The shell fragments inside the rock came out at about three thousand five hundred radiocarbon years.

49
00:03:52,002 --> 00:03:58,966
The natural cement that glues those shells together came out younger, at about two thousand eight hundred.

50
00:03:59,366 --> 00:04:00,615
That order matters.

51
00:04:00,615 --> 00:04:02,455
Shells lie on a beach first.

52
00:04:02,455 --> 00:04:04,822
The cement grows between them later.

53
00:04:04,822 --> 00:04:15,733
The dates came out in exactly that sequence, which is what you'd expect from a beach slowly turning into rock, and it suggests the samples weren't badly contaminated.

54
00:04:16,133 --> 00:04:22,357
A later Swiss study of Bimini's coast, by Eric Davaud and André Strasser, went further.

55
00:04:22,357 --> 00:04:31,800
They concluded this layer of rock formed beneath the island itself, and was only uncovered by erosion around two thousand years ago.

56
00:04:32,199 --> 00:04:37,843
Supporters of Atlantis point to a very different number: fifteen thousand years.

57
00:04:37,843 --> 00:04:42,500
It comes from a uranium-thorium test published in nineteen eighty.

58
00:04:42,899 --> 00:04:44,952
But that sample wasn't the road.

59
00:04:44,952 --> 00:04:51,108
It was the older limestone underneath, and it was partly recrystallised, which breaks the clock.

60
00:04:51,108 --> 00:04:56,302
And fifteen thousand years ago, sea level here was nearly a hundred metres lower.

61
00:04:56,302 --> 00:04:59,894
That limestone would have been a dry hill, not a seabed.

62
00:04:59,894 --> 00:05:01,433
The date can't be right.

63
00:05:01,833 --> 00:05:04,212
Prediction one fails for Atlantis.

64
00:05:04,212 --> 00:05:07,291
The rock is around three thousand years old.

65
00:05:07,291 --> 00:05:13,100
Younger than the pyramids of Giza, and many thousands of years too young for Plato.

66
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:17,045
Prediction two is the cleverest test in this whole story.

67
00:05:17,045 --> 00:05:19,533
And it came from a believer's challenge.

68
00:05:19,933 --> 00:05:24,466
In the mid-nineteen seventies, Eugene Shinn ran a small U.S.

69
00:05:24,466 --> 00:05:27,714
Geological Survey field station near Miami.

70
00:05:27,714 --> 00:05:32,474
His team had built a new underwater core drill, run by a diver.

71
00:05:32,474 --> 00:05:38,366
The writer Peter Tompkins, an Atlantis enthusiast, asked them to drill Bimini.

72
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:40,998
Their first cores said beachrock.

73
00:05:40,998 --> 00:05:42,960
Tompkins had an answer ready.

74
00:05:42,960 --> 00:05:48,033
What if beachrock was simply the only building material the Atlanteans had?

75
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:49,678
Here's the trick.

76
00:05:49,678 --> 00:05:55,246
Sand on a beach is laid down in thin layers that tilt gently toward the sea.

77
00:05:55,246 --> 00:06:01,399
When that sand turns to rock, the tilt is frozen inside, invisible from the outside.

78
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:09,634
So, if the blocks never moved since they formed, every one should show its layers dipping the same way, toward deep water.

79
00:06:09,634 --> 00:06:16,826
If people quarried them and fitted them together, they'd have chosen blocks by shape, not by an invisible grain.

80
00:06:16,826 --> 00:06:19,266
The dips should point every which way.

81
00:06:19,666 --> 00:06:25,001
The team drilled seventeen oriented cores from separate, neighbouring stones.

82
00:06:25,001 --> 00:06:30,266
Back at the lab, they sliced them with a diamond saw and x-rayed the slices.

83
00:06:30,666 --> 00:06:33,846
Every core dipped the same way, toward deep water.

84
00:06:33,846 --> 00:06:40,333
And distinctive layers of rounded beach pebbles could be traced from one stone straight into the next.

85
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:45,329
That's what one sheet of rock looks like after it cracks where it lies.

86
00:06:45,329 --> 00:06:47,271
Not a wall of separate blocks.

87
00:06:47,271 --> 00:06:50,119
And this test could have gone the other way.

88
00:06:50,119 --> 00:06:50,766
It didn't.

89
00:06:51,166 --> 00:06:54,017
It wasn't a quiet expedition, by the way.

90
00:06:54,017 --> 00:07:03,612
Shinn recalled naked boaters asking his divers whether they could feel the force field, and diviners flying over the site with plumb bobs.

91
00:07:03,612 --> 00:07:06,533
One believer later called him a scientoid.

92
00:07:06,933 --> 00:07:08,128
Prediction three.

93
00:07:08,128 --> 00:07:12,344
A real wall, road or breakwater has structure underneath it.

94
00:07:12,344 --> 00:07:17,966
A second layer of blocks, foundations, supports, something cut into the bedrock.

95
00:07:18,366 --> 00:07:25,067
A study funded by the National Geographic Society, by John Gifford and Mahlon Ball, went looking.

96
00:07:25,067 --> 00:07:29,212
They found no place where two layers of blocks were stacked.

97
00:07:29,212 --> 00:07:32,666
Not even one block set squarely on top of another.

98
00:07:33,066 --> 00:07:35,666
Bedrock lies just below the whole area.

99
00:07:35,666 --> 00:07:38,000
The inner blocks sit on loose sand.

100
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,199
Nothing was cut into the bedrock.

101
00:07:40,199 --> 00:07:49,199
No regular supports, no repeating grooves that could be tool marks, and not enough loose blocks around to have made a lost upper layer.

102
00:07:49,600 --> 00:07:58,549
Even David Zink, whose research was funded by the Cayce foundation, concluded that the blocks rest on bedrock or on small stones.

103
00:07:58,549 --> 00:08:04,100
So the visible stones were probably not the top of some larger buried structure.

104
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:07,833
Then, in two thousand five, a new team returned.

105
00:08:07,833 --> 00:08:20,750
The psychologist Greg Little and the researcher William Donato, backed by the Cayce association, reported blocks stacked in tiers, smaller prop stones wedged beneath them, and cut marks.

106
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:23,666
Their conclusion: an ancient harbour wall.

107
00:08:24,066 --> 00:08:25,596
Those dives were filmed.

108
00:08:25,596 --> 00:08:35,033
But the findings appeared in the team's own magazine and videos, not in a peer-reviewed journal where other scientists could check the measurements.

109
00:08:35,433 --> 00:08:37,995
Geology also has a mechanism for them.

110
00:08:37,995 --> 00:08:42,713
Under a thick bed of beachrock there are often thinner, broken layers.

111
00:08:42,713 --> 00:08:53,500
When waves scour the sand away, heavy slabs sink straight down until they hit hard rock, and the broken pieces get trapped underneath, looking a lot like props.

112
00:08:53,899 --> 00:09:02,952
And after fifty years of scientists drilling cores and tourists chipping souvenirs, fresh tool marks on a few blocks are guaranteed.

113
00:09:02,952 --> 00:09:04,666
They just aren't ancient.

114
00:09:05,066 --> 00:09:09,866
Prediction three: the structure a builder would leave behind is missing.

115
00:09:09,866 --> 00:09:12,533
The props remain a claim, not a finding.

116
00:09:12,933 --> 00:09:14,045
Prediction four.

117
00:09:14,045 --> 00:09:15,922
People leave things behind.

118
00:09:15,922 --> 00:09:21,899
Ancient harbours in the Mediterranean are full of pottery, tools, anchors and rubbish.

119
00:09:22,299 --> 00:09:25,255
And beachrock is very good at keeping them.

120
00:09:25,255 --> 00:09:32,333
On Bimini's own swimming beach, beachrock is forming today with soda and beer bottles cemented into it.

121
00:09:32,733 --> 00:09:39,005
In the Persian Gulf, Shinn found fresh beachrock with glass, pottery, nuts and bolts inside.

122
00:09:39,005 --> 00:09:47,799
At Loggerhead Key in Florida, an iron pipe laid for a research laboratory in the early nineteen hundreds is now part of the rock.

123
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:55,159
So if any civilization had worked on this beach, its debris should be locked inside these blocks.

124
00:09:55,159 --> 00:09:57,383
The cores and dives found none.

125
00:09:57,383 --> 00:09:59,966
No pottery, no tools, no wheel ruts.

126
00:10:00,366 --> 00:10:02,915
What about the famous Bimini columns?

127
00:10:02,915 --> 00:10:05,533
The marble pillars of a sunken temple?

128
00:10:05,933 --> 00:10:07,974
They lie about two miles away.

129
00:10:07,974 --> 00:10:20,629
In nineteen seventy-one, a letter in the journal Nature identified the cement ones: Portland cement that set inside the wooden barrels ships carried it in, back in the eighteen hundreds.

130
00:10:20,629 --> 00:10:21,718
The wood rotted.

131
00:10:21,718 --> 00:10:24,100
The cement kept the barrel's shape.

132
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,508
Stone anchors have turned up in the area too.

133
00:10:27,508 --> 00:10:29,514
They're real, and interesting.

134
00:10:29,514 --> 00:10:32,188
But anchors are what passing ships drop.

135
00:10:32,188 --> 00:10:36,000
They tell you about sailors, not about who made the rock.

136
00:10:36,399 --> 00:10:40,168
Prediction four: no ancient leftovers in the stones.

137
00:10:40,168 --> 00:10:44,299
The only artefacts nearby are thousands of years younger.

138
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:47,649
Prediction five is the believers' favourite.

139
00:10:47,649 --> 00:10:49,996
Nature doesn't draw straight lines.

140
00:10:49,996 --> 00:10:56,500
So, if this is natural, there shouldn't be other long, straight rows of squarish blocks anywhere.

141
00:10:56,899 --> 00:11:01,922
The geologist Paul Hearty answered that in a published debate with Donato.

142
00:11:01,922 --> 00:11:03,822
Beachrock follows the beach.

143
00:11:03,822 --> 00:11:08,166
As Shinn put it: if the beach is straight, the rock is straight.

144
00:11:08,566 --> 00:11:16,713
And when exposed beachrock bakes in the sun, it cracks like an old concrete road, into slabs up to twelve feet long.

145
00:11:16,713 --> 00:11:23,666
Waves and sand round off the corners until they look like giant loaves of bread, laid edge to edge.

146
00:11:24,066 --> 00:11:25,671
Then the matches start.

147
00:11:25,671 --> 00:11:37,466
In two thousand four and five, hurricanes Charley and Katrina stripped the sand from Loggerhead Key in the Dry Tortugas, and exposed a beachrock road just like Bimini's.

148
00:11:37,866 --> 00:11:47,695
There are more off Andros, Abaco and the Exumas, off Vieques in Puerto Rico, and around islands on Australia's Great Barrier Reef.

149
00:11:47,695 --> 00:11:54,500
In Tasmania, a naturally cracked tiled pavement at Eaglehawk Neck is a tourist attraction.

150
00:11:54,899 --> 00:12:08,600
And at Pulley Ridge, off southwest Florida, ninety metres down, survey teams found the same pillow-shaped blocks on a drowned barrier island, sunk by the rising sea at the end of the Ice Age.

151
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:16,660
Shinn even photographed fractured, road-like bedding from the submersible Alvin, five thousand feet down.

152
00:12:16,660 --> 00:12:19,433
Nobody suggests Atlanteans paved that.

153
00:12:19,833 --> 00:12:22,291
Prediction five fails for Atlantis.

154
00:12:22,291 --> 00:12:25,241
Long, straight rows of blocks aren't rare.

155
00:12:25,241 --> 00:12:27,700
They're simply what beachrock does.

156
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:29,547
The last prediction.

157
00:12:29,547 --> 00:12:34,033
The road ends in a curve, a hook shaped like an upside-down J.

158
00:12:34,033 --> 00:12:39,966
For believers, a hook means a harbour: a curved breakwater built to shelter ships.

159
00:12:40,366 --> 00:12:41,839
But beaches curve too.

160
00:12:41,839 --> 00:12:46,860
Where sand drifts along a coast, it builds a curved spit, like a fish hook.

161
00:12:46,860 --> 00:12:56,566
Shinn studied hook-shaped spits on the coast of Qatar in nineteen sixty-seven, and found beachrock forming on all of them, curving with the sand.

162
00:12:56,966 --> 00:13:02,626
The drowned barrier island at Pulley Ridge has its own J at its northern end.

163
00:13:02,626 --> 00:13:08,433
Hooks like it form near tidal inlets all along the coasts of the United States.

164
00:13:08,833 --> 00:13:17,066
Bimini would probably have a modern hook at its harbour entrance too, if a seawall and a boat channel hadn't been built right over it.

165
00:13:17,466 --> 00:13:19,830
Others have tried the hook as well.

166
00:13:19,830 --> 00:13:27,935
One popular book claimed a Chinese treasure fleet built it in fourteen twenty-one, as a slipway for storm-damaged ships.

167
00:13:27,935 --> 00:13:30,366
There's no evidence for that either.

168
00:13:30,766 --> 00:13:34,299
Prediction six: the hook fits a beach better than a harbour.

169
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:36,402
Seven predictions tested.

170
00:13:36,402 --> 00:13:37,899
Here is the scorecard.

171
00:13:38,299 --> 00:13:43,550
Cores show beachrock, the same rock forming on Bimini's beaches today: evidence.

172
00:13:43,550 --> 00:13:46,504
Bedding dips seaward in every core: evidence.

173
00:13:46,504 --> 00:13:54,839
Radiocarbon puts the shells at about three thousand five hundred years, and the cement at two thousand eight hundred: evidence.

174
00:13:54,839 --> 00:13:59,499
Identical roads at the Dry Tortugas, Andros and Pulley Ridge: evidence.

175
00:13:59,899 --> 00:14:04,298
Atlantis rising in sixty-eight and sixty-nine: no evidence.

176
00:14:04,298 --> 00:14:09,814
A second course, prop stones and tool marks as proof of masonry: unproven.

177
00:14:09,814 --> 00:14:16,299
And when the stones were first seen, in nineteen sixty-eight or earlier: still debated.

178
00:14:16,700 --> 00:14:17,508
The verdict.

179
00:14:17,508 --> 00:14:27,886
The Bimini Road is natural beachrock: a beach that turned to stone about three thousand years ago, cracked where it lay, and slipped under the rising sea.

180
00:14:27,886 --> 00:14:32,266
It is not a highway to Atlantis, and nothing rose in sixty-eight.

181
00:14:32,666 --> 00:14:34,713
But look at what it really is.

182
00:14:34,713 --> 00:14:38,466
A beach from three thousand years ago, frozen in stone.

183
00:14:38,466 --> 00:14:45,153
And a perfect lesson in how to test a claim: make a prediction that could fail, then go and check.

184
00:14:45,153 --> 00:14:47,405
A prophecy never takes that risk.

185
00:14:47,405 --> 00:14:48,633
A core drill does.

186
00:14:49,033 --> 00:14:50,974
Which prediction convinced you?

187
00:14:50,974 --> 00:14:56,233
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for the next ancient mystery put to the test.
